WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:01.901
"Adding oil to pasta water

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:04.320
prevents the noodles from sticking."

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.781
This is definitely not true.

00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:12.287
Hi, I'm Catherine DeOrio.

00:00:12.287 --> 00:00:13.455
I'm a formally trained chef;

00:00:13.455 --> 00:00:16.041
I trained here in Chicago
at Kendall College.

00:00:16.041 --> 00:00:19.002
Worked at various restaurants,
staging in Chicago,

00:00:19.002 --> 00:00:22.630
and I am here to debunk the
most common myths about cooking.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:25.258
We're doing this via Skype,
because, like many of you,

00:00:25.258 --> 00:00:26.968
I am stuck in my house as well,

00:00:26.968 --> 00:00:29.220
and I know all of you will
want some tips on how to cook

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.890
while you're indoors and
trying to find things to do.

00:00:33.516 --> 00:00:36.186
"Only flip your steak
once during cooking."

00:00:36.186 --> 00:00:37.729
This is absolutely not true.

00:00:37.729 --> 00:00:39.773
A lot of people say that because

00:00:39.773 --> 00:00:41.608
where all your flavor
comes is from the browning,

00:00:41.608 --> 00:00:43.777
so people feel that if
you're flipping your steak,

00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:45.153
you're not gonna get a proper brown

00:00:45.153 --> 00:00:46.780
and then all of that flavor.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:48.823
But the most important
thing for a good steak

00:00:48.823 --> 00:00:50.742
is actually the temperature of your pan.

00:00:50.742 --> 00:00:52.535
You make sure you get it really hot,

00:00:52.535 --> 00:00:55.455
and you can actually
flip it multiple times.

00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:57.707
So, if you flip it about every 30 seconds,

00:00:57.707 --> 00:01:00.668
it's going to not cool down on the top,

00:01:00.668 --> 00:01:03.046
so it will continue to
cook, you'll flip it,

00:01:03.046 --> 00:01:05.381
it will cook, and then it's almost like

00:01:05.381 --> 00:01:08.176
being cooked simultaneously on both sides,

00:01:08.176 --> 00:01:09.594
so you're actually gonna get

00:01:09.594 --> 00:01:11.387
a faster cooking time on it,

00:01:11.387 --> 00:01:13.223
and it will consistently cook

00:01:13.223 --> 00:01:14.891
from edge to edge of the steak.

00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:17.644
"Adding vinegar to your cooking water

00:01:17.644 --> 00:01:20.480
makes your hard-boiled
eggs easier to peel."

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.856
That's not exactly true.

00:01:21.856 --> 00:01:23.691
It's kind of a placebo effect.

00:01:23.691 --> 00:01:25.151
So, a few things.

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:27.237
The age of your egg is gonna affect

00:01:27.237 --> 00:01:29.030
how easy it is to peel.

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:30.907
So, an older egg is more alkaline.

00:01:30.907 --> 00:01:32.075
That's gonna make it a
little easier to peel

00:01:32.075 --> 00:01:33.409
after it's boiled.

00:01:33.409 --> 00:01:35.161
The next thing is, it's really important,

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:36.871
is to stop that cooking right away

00:01:36.871 --> 00:01:38.915
and submerge it in an ice bath.

00:01:38.915 --> 00:01:41.417
So, nothing fancy, just a bowl of water

00:01:41.417 --> 00:01:43.419
with a bunch of ice in it, nice and cold,

00:01:43.419 --> 00:01:45.505
submerge it in it, let the cooking stop,

00:01:45.505 --> 00:01:47.590
and then pull them out.

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:49.300
The other way you could get the shell

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.011
to not stick is by steaming them.

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:54.764
So, what happens when you steam the egg

00:01:54.764 --> 00:01:57.809
is the proteins from your egg whites

00:01:57.809 --> 00:02:00.186
are gonna separate from the
membrane from the shell.

00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:01.604
It's gonna give you a little bit of space,

00:02:01.604 --> 00:02:04.107
therefore making it easy
to peel when you're done.

00:02:05.608 --> 00:02:08.069
"Wear goggles, chew gum, etc.,"

00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:10.029
all of the myths you've heard to avoid

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:11.447
your eyes from getting watery

00:02:11.447 --> 00:02:13.241
when you're cutting onions.

00:02:13.241 --> 00:02:14.075
Um,

00:02:14.075 --> 00:02:14.909
no.

00:02:14.909 --> 00:02:17.871
So, I guess you could wear goggles,

00:02:17.871 --> 00:02:19.581
aside from making you look very strange,

00:02:19.581 --> 00:02:22.750
but the reason our eyes water

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:24.752
when we cut an onion is

00:02:24.752 --> 00:02:26.546
there is an enzymatic reaction,

00:02:26.546 --> 00:02:29.174
and the gases come up from the onion,

00:02:29.174 --> 00:02:30.925
and that's actually
what gets into our eyes.

00:02:30.925 --> 00:02:33.344
It causes them to burn, and
it causes them to water.

00:02:33.344 --> 00:02:36.472
So, interestingly, that gas comes out

00:02:36.472 --> 00:02:39.350
because it's a defense
mechanism for the onion

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:40.393
so little critters underground,

00:02:40.393 --> 00:02:42.312
as it's growing, don't eat it.

00:02:42.312 --> 00:02:45.356
The best way to really

00:02:45.356 --> 00:02:47.650
mitigate the gases coming out

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:49.736
is to use a really sharp knife.

00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:51.654
So, if you use a dull knife,

00:02:51.654 --> 00:02:54.532
you're crushing the membrane of the onion,

00:02:54.532 --> 00:02:57.619
and it's gonna cause more gas to release.

00:02:57.619 --> 00:02:59.621
If you cut it really,

00:02:59.621 --> 00:03:01.331
just with a really sharp knife

00:03:01.331 --> 00:03:03.082
and you slice through it,

00:03:03.082 --> 00:03:05.710
it's going to damage
less of the membranes,

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:07.253
and so less gas is gonna come up,

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:08.963
so it's gonna be a little easier.

00:03:08.963 --> 00:03:11.049
Also, cut the root last.

00:03:11.049 --> 00:03:13.801
A lot of gas is kind of
concentrated in that area,

00:03:13.801 --> 00:03:15.929
so kind of that little
hairy part at the end,

00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:19.432
so to speak, always cut that off last.

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:22.352
"Whipped cream flattens
when you stop whipping it."

00:03:22.352 --> 00:03:23.853
So, that's not true.

00:03:23.853 --> 00:03:28.233
It's not the whipping that will
keep the air in your cream,

00:03:28.233 --> 00:03:31.402
which makes it fluffy, it's
actually your temperature.

00:03:31.402 --> 00:03:34.447
So, again, temperature
is really important.

00:03:34.447 --> 00:03:37.492
If it gets warm, that's
when your whipped cream

00:03:37.492 --> 00:03:38.952
starts to flatten.

00:03:38.952 --> 00:03:40.703
So the tip is,

00:03:40.703 --> 00:03:42.747
before you whip it,

00:03:42.747 --> 00:03:44.624
don't only chill your cream, which,

00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:46.876
obviously you wanna get
that as cold as possible,

00:03:46.876 --> 00:03:49.003
but I like to put my bowl and my whisk

00:03:49.003 --> 00:03:50.838
or whisk attachment in the freezer,

00:03:50.838 --> 00:03:52.548
get it really cold,

00:03:52.548 --> 00:03:55.301
so when you whip it then it stays cold,

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:57.345
it won't flatten during
the whipping process,

00:03:57.345 --> 00:03:58.263
and then when you're done,

00:03:58.263 --> 00:03:59.973
you can store it in the fridge.

00:03:59.973 --> 00:04:01.599
If you're going to go to a party,

00:04:01.599 --> 00:04:02.850
let's just say you make a cake,

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:03.893
and you frost it with a cream

00:04:03.893 --> 00:04:05.728
and there's a whipped cream element to it,

00:04:05.728 --> 00:04:07.814
you could stick it in the
freezer for about 30 minutes.

00:04:07.814 --> 00:04:09.440
It will make it a little more stable

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:12.318
if you're, for instance,
taking it to a picnic

00:04:12.318 --> 00:04:14.279
or somewhere where
you're gonna be outside,

00:04:14.279 --> 00:04:15.405
it's gonna be a little warm.

00:04:15.405 --> 00:04:17.615
That will help to keep the whipped cream

00:04:17.615 --> 00:04:19.242
nice and airy and fluffy.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:23.037
"Adding oil to pasta water prevents

00:04:23.037 --> 00:04:24.956
the noodles from sticking."

00:04:24.956 --> 00:04:27.292
This is definitely not true.

00:04:27.292 --> 00:04:29.919
As an Italian, this is just the one thing

00:04:29.919 --> 00:04:31.879
that just really irks me.

00:04:31.879 --> 00:04:34.507
So, if you put oil on your noodles,

00:04:34.507 --> 00:04:36.175
it's actually gonna make the surface

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:38.928
of the noodles impenetrable to your sauce.

00:04:38.928 --> 00:04:42.181
So, the thing that's gonna
give your noodles flavor

00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:43.474
is not gonna be able to get in

00:04:43.474 --> 00:04:45.643
and actually flavor them
or stick to your noodles.

00:04:45.643 --> 00:04:49.439
So, that's kind of not
what you want, right?

00:04:49.439 --> 00:04:51.733
Pasta is, even, like, in Italy,

00:04:51.733 --> 00:04:53.484
the shapes of the pasta are designed

00:04:53.484 --> 00:04:56.237
to hold certain kinds
of sauces a certain way.

00:04:56.237 --> 00:04:59.073
So the last thing you want is, you know,

00:04:59.073 --> 00:05:01.951
these slick noodles where all
of your sauce is coming out.

00:05:01.951 --> 00:05:05.204
So the best way to get
noodles to stop from sticking

00:05:05.204 --> 00:05:09.876
is, again, add your
pasta to boiling water,

00:05:09.876 --> 00:05:12.587
make sure it's a full, rolling boil,

00:05:12.587 --> 00:05:14.505
and then stir it every once in a while.

00:05:14.505 --> 00:05:16.674
Make sure that you're continually stirring

00:05:16.674 --> 00:05:18.509
that pasta as it's cooking.

00:05:18.509 --> 00:05:21.054
This is gonna keep it
from sticking together

00:05:21.054 --> 00:05:23.097
during the cooking process.

00:05:23.097 --> 00:05:24.807
"You can season food at any time

00:05:24.807 --> 00:05:26.434
during the cooking process."

00:05:26.434 --> 00:05:28.770
No, no, no, no, no. [laughs]

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:30.938
Season as you go.

00:05:30.938 --> 00:05:34.359
So, if you just season at the end,

00:05:34.359 --> 00:05:35.818
all you're gonna have is kind of a

00:05:35.818 --> 00:05:37.528
superficial seasoning.

00:05:37.528 --> 00:05:39.280
There's gonna be,

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:41.532
the top layer of your food

00:05:41.532 --> 00:05:43.618
is gonna have some flavor to it,

00:05:43.618 --> 00:05:45.453
but it's not gonna have actually

00:05:45.453 --> 00:05:47.080
penetrated your food and give you

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:49.415
that kind of consistent flavor throughout

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:51.417
and that depth and complexity,

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:52.710
which is what you really love.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:55.213
And so that's why a lot of times

00:05:55.213 --> 00:05:56.214
you'll make something at home

00:05:56.214 --> 00:05:57.507
and you'll just kind
of at the end be like,

00:05:57.507 --> 00:05:59.300
oh, season to taste, and just throw some

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:01.803
salt and pepper on it, maybe some herbs,

00:06:01.803 --> 00:06:03.137
and you're like, "Why didn't this turn out

00:06:03.137 --> 00:06:05.348
the way it tastes at a restaurant?

00:06:05.348 --> 00:06:06.891
It's a simple dish."

00:06:06.891 --> 00:06:10.770
But it's because you are
constantly seasoning as you go.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:13.106
"Milk makes fluffier scrambled eggs."

00:06:13.106 --> 00:06:14.190
No, no, no.

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:16.901
So, scrambled eggs,
it's about temperature.

00:06:16.901 --> 00:06:20.363
It's about, think slow and low.

00:06:20.363 --> 00:06:23.491
So you want low, medium heat,

00:06:23.491 --> 00:06:26.160
and you wanna just cook those eggs slow,

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:30.123
and just once they start to coagulate,

00:06:30.123 --> 00:06:32.333
take a spatula and move it around

00:06:32.333 --> 00:06:33.709
so you'll keep getting those curds.

00:06:33.709 --> 00:06:35.461
If you add milk to it,

00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:37.463
that milk is gonna
separate out from the eggs

00:06:37.463 --> 00:06:39.757
as it's cooking, and you're gonna probably

00:06:39.757 --> 00:06:42.427
get rubbery, colorless,

00:06:42.427 --> 00:06:44.762
a diluted flavor on your actual eggs.

00:06:44.762 --> 00:06:47.056
So, slow and low.

00:06:47.056 --> 00:06:50.935
Also, before, kind of almost right before

00:06:50.935 --> 00:06:52.812
your eggs seem totally cooked,

00:06:52.812 --> 00:06:54.564
so they're not runny anymore

00:06:54.564 --> 00:06:57.525
but they seem a little wet, take them off.

00:06:57.525 --> 00:06:59.026
There is gonna be some carryover cooking,

00:06:59.026 --> 00:07:01.612
and they're gonna cook a
little bit more in that pan

00:07:01.612 --> 00:07:03.948
as you're transferring
them to your serving dish.

00:07:03.948 --> 00:07:06.868
"Sticking a potato in an overly salty dish

00:07:06.868 --> 00:07:10.371
will absorb the salt
and make it less salty."

00:07:11.289 --> 00:07:13.040
No. Again, no.

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:15.793
So, yes, will putting a raw potato

00:07:15.793 --> 00:07:19.046
into an overly salted soup

00:07:19.046 --> 00:07:20.214
absorb some of that salt?

00:07:20.214 --> 00:07:22.633
Yes, but it's also gonna absorb

00:07:22.633 --> 00:07:25.094
a disproportionate amount of the liquid,

00:07:25.094 --> 00:07:27.054
so you don't want to do that.

00:07:27.054 --> 00:07:30.183
The first thing you
should try before anything

00:07:30.183 --> 00:07:32.727
is try to balance out the flavors.

00:07:32.727 --> 00:07:36.689
So if it's saltier,
maybe just a hit of acid,

00:07:36.689 --> 00:07:39.108
so a little dash of sherry vinegar

00:07:39.108 --> 00:07:42.236
or a little bit of a squeeze of lemon

00:07:42.236 --> 00:07:44.363
might balance it out, a hit of sugar.

00:07:44.363 --> 00:07:45.656
So, just kind of think.

00:07:45.656 --> 00:07:47.617
What makes something, a dish, taste good

00:07:47.617 --> 00:07:50.369
is that balance, that
sweet and salty and spicy.

00:07:50.369 --> 00:07:52.497
So just see what it might need.

00:07:52.497 --> 00:07:53.498
Try that first.

00:07:53.498 --> 00:07:55.958
If it doesn't work, move on.

00:07:55.958 --> 00:07:59.462
So, "storing bread in the
fridge prevents staleness."

00:07:59.462 --> 00:08:01.547
Actually, storing bread in the fridge

00:08:01.547 --> 00:08:03.674
will make your bread go stale quicker.

00:08:03.674 --> 00:08:06.344
So, it's gonna pull some
of that moisture out.

00:08:06.344 --> 00:08:09.805
But there are ways that
you can keep your bread

00:08:09.805 --> 00:08:10.932
for a bit longer.

00:08:10.932 --> 00:08:13.476
So, one, you can freeze it.

00:08:13.476 --> 00:08:15.853
Wrap it in plastic and then freeze it

00:08:15.853 --> 00:08:17.605
and then defrost it at room temperature,

00:08:17.605 --> 00:08:19.982
and your bread will pretty much stay

00:08:19.982 --> 00:08:21.859
close to what it was when you bought it.

00:08:21.859 --> 00:08:24.779
Now, if the bread's already gone stale,

00:08:24.779 --> 00:08:26.447
is there anything you can do?

00:08:26.447 --> 00:08:27.823
So, yes, there's a few things.

00:08:27.823 --> 00:08:28.741
If it's already gone stale,

00:08:28.741 --> 00:08:30.618
one, just heating the bread a little bit

00:08:30.618 --> 00:08:32.620
will actually soften it up.

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:35.456
Also, you can put that
bread in the fridge.

00:08:35.456 --> 00:08:37.542
Now, when you take it out of the fridge,

00:08:37.542 --> 00:08:38.543
it's not that it's gonna

00:08:38.543 --> 00:08:40.545
be back to its original state,

00:08:40.545 --> 00:08:42.880
but it will just keep it edible

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:43.881
for a little bit longer.

00:08:43.881 --> 00:08:46.634
It will keep it from
going bad or going moldy.

00:08:46.634 --> 00:08:48.719
"Keeping a pit in your avocado

00:08:48.719 --> 00:08:51.472
keeps the avocado from browning."

00:08:51.472 --> 00:08:55.268
Again, no, this is not accurate.

00:08:55.268 --> 00:08:59.355
It will keep the avocado
underneath the pit from browning.

00:08:59.355 --> 00:09:00.982
So, the browning process happens,

00:09:00.982 --> 00:09:04.068
it's an enzymatic reaction that causes

00:09:04.068 --> 00:09:07.572
the flesh of the fruit to brown.

00:09:07.572 --> 00:09:11.701
The best way for a full avocado

00:09:11.701 --> 00:09:14.996
is to either swipe some lemon juice on it

00:09:14.996 --> 00:09:17.164
or some type of acidulated water.

00:09:17.164 --> 00:09:18.874
Acidulated water is basically just water

00:09:18.874 --> 00:09:20.376
that has some acid in it.

00:09:20.376 --> 00:09:23.754
You could also do a swipe of olive oil.

00:09:23.754 --> 00:09:25.965
That will keep it for a little while,

00:09:25.965 --> 00:09:29.343
'cause again that's gonna
create a seal on the top.

00:09:29.343 --> 00:09:31.762
"Cooking with frozen
vegetables doesn't provide

00:09:31.762 --> 00:09:35.308
the same nutrients and
flavor as fresh vegetables."

00:09:35.308 --> 00:09:37.226
Absolutely wrong. In fact,

00:09:37.226 --> 00:09:38.853
in some cases you'll find

00:09:38.853 --> 00:09:42.732
that it is better to
buy frozen vegetables.

00:09:42.732 --> 00:09:43.691
Companies out there that obviously

00:09:43.691 --> 00:09:44.775
this is what they specialize in.

00:09:44.775 --> 00:09:46.444
So, Birds Eye, for instance,

00:09:46.444 --> 00:09:49.905
makes a variety of frozen vegetables,

00:09:49.905 --> 00:09:52.033
and they flash-freeze them

00:09:52.033 --> 00:09:54.535
so they're basically
frozen instantaneously,

00:09:54.535 --> 00:09:56.162
right after they're picked,

00:09:56.162 --> 00:09:59.707
so that is going to keep
all of the nutrients intact,

00:09:59.707 --> 00:10:02.710
their flavor, and this is
at their peak ripeness,

00:10:02.710 --> 00:10:04.920
so this is, like, the
best time you can get it.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:07.548
The other thing is, vegetables do start

00:10:07.548 --> 00:10:12.345
to degrade in their
nutrient content over time.

00:10:12.345 --> 00:10:15.222
Right, that's, nobody's
gonna argue with that.

00:10:15.222 --> 00:10:17.600
So there is quite a time, often,

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:19.393
between when it's picked

00:10:19.393 --> 00:10:20.936
and then when it's actually shipped

00:10:20.936 --> 00:10:23.189
and then when it's shelved
into your grocery store.

00:10:23.189 --> 00:10:24.774
So you do have to remember,

00:10:24.774 --> 00:10:26.359
even a thing that seems fresh

00:10:26.359 --> 00:10:30.446
has had a bit of a life
before it got to you.

00:10:30.446 --> 00:10:31.739
"Fresh tomatoes are better

00:10:31.739 --> 00:10:33.658
than canned tomatoes to cook with."

00:10:33.658 --> 00:10:36.118
One of my things that I
always have in my pantry

00:10:36.118 --> 00:10:37.411
are canned tomatoes,

00:10:37.411 --> 00:10:38.746
and the reason being is,

00:10:38.746 --> 00:10:40.873
so, OK, I love fresh tomatoes.

00:10:40.873 --> 00:10:43.959
It's, you know, late summer,

00:10:43.959 --> 00:10:46.253
and you get that beautiful garden tomato,

00:10:46.253 --> 00:10:49.590
and you can literally eat
it like an apple, right?

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:51.509
That season is about this long.

00:10:51.509 --> 00:10:53.177
Hunt's tomato takes the tomatoes,

00:10:53.177 --> 00:10:55.888
they can them at peak ripeness,

00:10:55.888 --> 00:10:57.348
so it's at tomato season,

